# Clothes on their hangers from the figure kit, with I wear and when it rains.
lesson language-clothes v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=9 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What I wear"
  goal "Name seven things to wear in [[language.name]], and say what you wear in what weather."

  look {
    scene 25x8 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row clothes space=1 align=end {
          garment a kind=top colour=sky sleeves=short print=none pattern=plain
          garment b kind=skirt colour=berry sleeves=short print=none pattern=plain
          garment c kind=trousers colour=mint sleeves=short print=none pattern=plain
          garment d kind=boots colour=tang sleeves=short print=none pattern=plain
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      To say what you wear, say [[clothes.i-am-wearing-a-t-shirt]], I am wearing a T-shirt. The
      weather decides what you wear: [[phrase.when-it-rains-i-wear-boots]], when it rains, I wear
      boots, and [[clothes.when-it-is-hot-i-wear-shorts]], when it is hot, I wear shorts.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.clothes-name k=3
    }
    show language.clothes-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.clothes-meaning k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
    show language.clothes-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.clothes-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.clothes-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.clothes-write k=3
    show language.clothes-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.clothes-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.clothes-write k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.clothes-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.clothes-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[clothes.i-am-wearing-a-t-shirt]] I am wearing a T-shirt. [[phrase.when-it-rains-i-wear-boots]] When it rains, I wear boots."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the trousers and the shorts taken for each other, and the dress
    and the skirt: every naming question offers the one most often mixed up. The clothes are cut
    from the shelf's figure kit and hang the same way every time, so the picture carries the answer
    to the naming questions and the child's job is the word. The step up from the weather lesson of
    this grade is that the weather now decides a sentence of the child's own, joined with when. The
    listening question is a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as
    Read aloud, or let the device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts
    there, while the words typed on their own in this lesson are taken without their accents. If the
    child is stuck, lay out real clothes and name each as it goes on. Off the page, say what to wear
    each morning in the language, after looking at the sky. It meets England's key stage 2 languages
    programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and actions
    orally and in writing"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities: "Activities to
    listen to simple words and phrases and basic expressions about their familiar people and their
    surroundings and match the phrases and expressions with illustrations and photographs"; and CEFR
    A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases
    are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[clothes.the-trousers]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[clothes.the-t-shirt]] and [[clothes.the-dress]] and the words to write are [[clothes.skirt]] and [[clothes.boots]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[clothes.the-shorts]] and [[clothes.the-shoes]] and the words to write are [[clothes.trousers]], [[clothes.boots]] and [[clothes.dress]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
